## Fixed Issues
  #XXXX - Fixed a SEGFAULT on PATCH of an attribute that states its type but not its value, when TRoE is enabled - e.g. PATCH /entities/{id}/attrs/{attr} with {"type": "Relationship", "observedAt": ...} and no "object". A merge patch does not have to restate what it is not changing, so pgAttributeBuild passed no value on, and every typed branch of pgAttributeAppend read it without checking. As with the sub-attribute crash, the response was already sent - TRoE runs from MHD's requestCompleted callback - so the client saw its 204 and the broker died a moment later. Such a row is now recorded the way an attribute with no type given already was: an 'Update' with no value. Omitting the type from the same payload was always harmless, which is why this went unnoticed
  #XXXX - Fixed a SEGFAULT on any PATCH that deletes a sub-attribute, when TRoE is enabled. troePatchEntity2 announced the deletion to pgSubAttributeAppend as a "String" while passing no value at all, and pgSubAttributeAppend dereferenced the NULL. The response was already on its way out - TRoE runs from MHD's requestCompleted callback - so the client saw its 204 and the broker died a moment later. A sub-attribute deletion is now not written to the temporal database at all, which is what already happened in practice (the broker died before issuing the INSERT, and even had it not, the row was malformed and postgres rejected the whole statement, losing the rest of the PATCH with it). Recording such a deletion is not possible in the current schema: unlike 'attributes', the 'subAttributes' table has no opMode column, and its 'valueType' enum has no "Delete" member. pgSubAttributeAppend now also refuses a value-less call with a log line instead of a crash
  #XXXX - Fixed GET /ngsi-ld/v1/types hanging forever when the broker runs with -mongocOnly and there are no entities in the database. Not slow - no response at all, the connection simply stayed open. dbEntityTypesGet asks mongoc for the types and falls back to the legacy C++ driver when it gets NULL back, which is what an empty entities collection returns; but -mongocOnly deliberately never initialises that driver, so the call blocked on a connection pool that does not exist. A single entity in the database was enough to hide it, so it only ever showed up on a freshly created one. With -mongocOnly the legacy driver is now never called, and the 'legacy' request header cannot ask for a driver that is not there
  #XXXX - Fixed the numbering of subordinate subscriptions (distributed subscriptions): the sequence number appended to the parent's Subscription ID was incremented once per subordinate ALREADY created, so a subscription matching three registrations created "<id>:1", "<id>:2" and "<id>:4", and a fourth got ":6". The ids are now consecutive
  #XXXX - The 'success' array of the 207 Multi-Status response of a batch delete is now in a deterministic order. With a distributed operation it was merged in the order the registrants happened to answer, so two identical requests gave two different responses. Same reasoning as for 'notUpdated' below - the array is a set, the order of its items carries no meaning
  #XXXX - The 'notUpdated' array of a 207 Multi-Status response is now in a deterministic order. Both arrays of that response are sets - the order of their items carries no meaning - and with a distributed operation the order was simply the order in which the registrants answered, so two identical requests gave two different responses. 'updated' was already sorted; 'notUpdated' now is too
  #XXXX - A Subscription created over a WebSocket now names its connection in 'notification::endpoint::uri' - "urn:ngsi-ld:ws:<file descriptor>" instead of the "ws://ws-placeholder:0" it used to be given, which read like a field somebody forgot to fill in. The file descriptor is what tells two WS subscriptions apart, and it is also carried in the Host header of the notifications sent over that WebSocket
  #XXXX - GET /subscriptions now renders 'notification::timesFailed' (NGSI-LD spec clause 5, NotificationParams). It was only rendered when the subscription was read with options=fromDb
  #XXXX - GET /subscriptions now renders 'geoQ::coordinates' as a JSON Array in all cases. After a PATCH of the geoQ, the coordinates were rendered as a String until the next broker restart
  #XXXX - An unreachable @context is now answered with 504 Gateway Timeout, not 503 Service Unavailable: the NGSI-LD spec maps LdContextNotAvailable to 504 (TS 104-176 clause 6, "Mapping of error types to HTTP status codes"), and 503 was the earlier value
  #XXXX - Fixed subscription over-matching when the 'entities' array holds more than one entity selector: an entity matching one selector's id/idPattern and a DIFFERENT selector's type was taken for a match. Each selector is now evaluated as a whole - id (or idPattern) AND type - the array being an OR of the selectors, per NGSI-LD spec clause 5.2.12
  #XXXX - Fixed relative references inside an "@context" array: per RFC 3986 clause 5 a String inside an "@context" array is an IRI *reference* and is resolved against the URL of the document that carries it. Previously such an entry was taken as-is, so a served or remote @context using relative references (e.g. "user-context.jsonld") failed to load
  #XXXX - Fixed an @context's own URL being confused with the reference it points to, which made a later lookup of that @context resolve to the wrong document
  #XXXX - TRoE: a failing SQL statement was silently dropped - no log entry, the transaction aborted, and COMMIT acted as ROLLBACK, so the temporal write was lost without a trace. Such errors are now logged (status, Postgres message and the offending statement) and the transaction is rolled back explicitly
  #1943 - Fixed crash on a malformed q-filter with a missing AND/OR separator between two expressions (e.g. q=category=="office"category!="inactive" - missing ';'): qLexCheck did not validate the token following a String/Boolean/Integer/Float value, so the malformed token stream reached qParse and triggered a NULL dereference. Such input is now rejected with 400 Bad Request
  #1899 - Fixed broker startup stall on a cyclic @context in the persisted context cache: at startup the cached @contexts are reloaded, and an array-@context re-downloads its referenced contexts recursively. A context that references itself (directly or via a chain) made the loader wait the full download timeout (3s) per cycle in contextCacheWait - which exists to let one thread wait for ANOTHER thread already downloading the same context, but during the single-threaded startup recursion the "other downloader" is the same thread, so the wait could never be satisfied (and with enough cross-referencing contexts the cumulative stall trips orchestrator startup probes -> CrashLoop). The recursion now detects same-thread re-entry and rejects the cyclic @context immediately. Such non-fatal context-load failures are now logged as warnings instead of errors, so they no longer look like a failed startup
  #XXXX - Batch delete (non-legacy path): a duplicated Entity ID is now handled per NGSI-LD spec clause 10.3.6 - the first occurrence is deleted and each subsequent occurrence gets a ResourceNotFound (404) error in the 207 response (the duplicates were previously dropped silently)
  #1951 - Fixed slow, half-broken batch upsert of more than 1000 entities: the legacy "which of these already exist" lookup (mongoCppLegacyEntityListLookupWithIdTypeCreDate) truncated its result at a hardcoded 1000, so on a re-upsert the surplus entities were misclassified as new and re-inserted (duplicate-key path) instead of updated. The lookup's $in query is already bounded by the batch (and thus by the max payload size), so the arbitrary 1000 cap was removed
  #1943 - Fixed heap corruption / crash under concurrent load: the MHD request thread pool (enabled by reqPoolSize, recently turned on by default) runs MHD in epoll mode, multiplexing several connections onto each pool thread - but the in-progress request state (payload buffer, kalloc, requestTree) lives in the thread-local orionldState, so concurrent (large, multi-chunk) requests corrupted each other (heap use-after-free / "free(): invalid size"). The MHD thread pool is now disabled: the broker always runs thread-per-connection. The -reqPoolSize/TRQ_POOL_SIZE option is still accepted (so existing deployments start) but is ignored and logs a warning
  #XXXX - Fixed entity corruption from PATCH /entities/{id}/attrs carrying a body datasetId: the datasetId instance was moved aside but never written, and the emptied attribute clobbered the stored default instance (the next read 500'd). PATCH /attrs does not implement datasetId instances - it is now rejected with 501 and the entity is left untouched
  #XXXX - Fixed stale value in notifications when PATCH /entities/{id} (merge) updates an EXISTING datasetId instance: the notification render only folded the patched dataset instances in when the attribute had no instances in the DB yet, so an update notified the old DB value (and a DDS action's terminal notification dropped its ddsActionResult entirely). The patched instances are now merged into the render entity in all cases
  #1905 - TRoE: segfault (exit 139) connecting to TimescaleDB Cloud - added SSL support and fixed NULL pointer crash
  #XXXX - Fixed crash under concurrent notification delivery by replacing thread-unsafe gethostbyname with getaddrinfo in orionldServerConnect
  #XXXX - Fixed query of many large entities (cfreyfh - Carsten Freyh at Fraunhofer IKTS)
  #XXXX - Wait for postgres instead of exiting after the first try - just like for mongo (1 try per second during 30 secs)
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in relationship endpoint when no relations found (cfreyfh)
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in GET /types when entity has empty attrNames (NULL attr name from MongoDB aggregation)
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in POST /entityOperations/query when entities array contains non-object items
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in batch update when entity payload contains "scope" field
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in PATCH entity when entity doesn't exist locally but distOps are active
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in TRoE for POST /entities with distOps returning 207 (requestTree was NULL)
  #XXXX - Fixed TRoE crash on POST /temporal/entities with NULL requestTree
  #XXXX - Fixed hang on POST /temporal/entities when using -mongocOnly (legacy mongoEntityExists blocked on semaphore)
  #XXXX - Fixed duplicate key error log for context cache persistence (ignore already-persisted contexts)
  #XXXX - Fixed default MQTT port not set when URI has no explicit port (now defaults to 1883/8883)
  #XXXX - Fixed "scope" not skipped in distOpResponses success attribute list
  #XXXX - Fixed Location header for POST /jsonldContexts to use relative path (per ETSI API spec)
  #XXXX - Fixed crash in pernot notification with simplified format (NULL serviceP dereference)
  #XXXX - Fixed Ngsild-Attribute-Format header in pernot notifications (was hardcoded to Normalized)
  #XXXX - Fixed Link header scoping bug in pernot notifications (linkHeader buffer out of scope)
  #XXXX - Fixed pernot GET rendering to use actual isActive/status instead of hardcoded true/active
  #XXXX - Fixed broker abort on requests that allocate more than 1000 buffers needing delayed free (e.g. large TRoE batch operations)

## Modified Behaviour
  #XXXX - The notification counters and timestamps of on-change subscriptions ('timesSent', 'timesFailed', 'lastNotification', 'lastSuccess', 'lastFailure') now reach the database on a timer as well, controlled by the existing -subCacheFlushIval (default 10 seconds), which until now only flushed periodic-notification subscriptions. They were pushed only when a subscription had -cSubCounters notifications pending (default 20), so the counters of a subscription notifying less often than that stayed in the broker's memory - invisible to a second broker instance sharing the database, and lost on restart. The in-memory values, which is what GET /subscriptions renders, are unchanged
  #XXXX - GET /cache/statistics (and /admin/cache/statistics) no longer reports "refresh". That counter counted the reloads of the sub-cache-refresh mechanism, which reloaded the whole cache from the database on a timer; the subscription cache is now maintained one item at a time, as subscriptions are created, patched and deleted, so there is nothing to count. "ids", "inserts", "removes", "updates" and "items" are unchanged, and are now reported for the new subscription cache

## New Features
  #XXXX - High Availability: several broker instances sharing one MongoDB now keep their subscription, registration and @context caches in step with each other, over mongo change streams. New CLI option -ha (env: HA): '-ha mongo' uses change streams and needs the database to be a REPLICA SET (a single node is enough) - a standalone mongod has no oplog and the broker refuses to start rather than run a sync that never fires. An '-ha <ip:port>' names an haaux instance, the channel meant for deployments not backed by mongo; it is parsed but not implemented yet. Without -ha nothing changes: no thread is started and no watch is opened. Until now a second instance never learned of the first one's subscriptions at all unless -subCacheIval was set, and then only by re-reading the whole collection on a timer
  #XXXX - The default core @context is now v1.9 (was v1.8), which is what the current ETSI TTF targets. Both versions remain selectable with -coreContext (v1.9 could not be selected at all before). Two terms are affected by the version change: "geometryProperty" is gone from the core @context (it is a URL parameter, never a payload member, and URL parameters are not @context-expanded), and "objectsLists" was renamed to "objectLists" - a breaking change only for the simplified temporal representation of a ListRelationship. The everyday singular "objectList" is unchanged
  #XXXX - New CLI option -migrate for the TRoE Postgres schema. The broker no longer migrates an existing database silently: at startup it checks the schema version, and an outdated database makes it log "back up your DB and restart with -migrate" and exit. A brand-new database is stamped at the current version and starts normally (Carsten Frey, Fraunhofer IKTS)
  #XXXX - Write correlator: every write now carries a correlator identifying the operation, taken from the new "NGSILD-Correlator" request header (falling back to "Fiware-Correlator"), or generated as "urn:ngsi-ld:correlator:<uuid>" if neither is present. It is stored on every TRoE row (entities, attributes, subAttributes) in a new "correlator" column, linking all attributes written by one operation - something instanceId, observedAt and ts cannot provide. The entity's "lastCorrelator" in MongoDB is only set when the client actually supplied a correlator, so behaviour without the header is unchanged (Carsten Frey, Fraunhofer IKTS)
  #XXXX - Native NGSI-LD temporal query support: GET/POST for temporal entities with:
          q-filter, geo-queries, aggregation, pick/omit, datasetId, and temporalValues
          — replacing previous Mintaka-based stubs
  #XXXX - Implemented PATCH for periodic notification (pernot) subscriptions
  #XXXX - Subscriptions support a top-level 'datasetId' (String or Array of String) that projects each
          notification body to the matching dataset instance(s) of every attribute, per NGSI-LD spec
          clause 8.5 (non-matching attributes are dropped; '@none' selects the default instance).
          Persisted, survives broker restart, and rendered back on GET
  #XXXX - Subscriptions support datasetId-scoped 'watchedAttributes' via the syntax "<attr>@<datasetId>"
          (Orion-LD extension, proposed to ETSI): the subscription is TRIGGERED only when an instance with
          that datasetId changes. The datasetId must be a URI; the token '@none' (i.e. "<attr>@@none") scopes
          to the default no-datasetId instance; a plain "<attr>" still matches any instance. Persisted and
          rendered back on GET
  #1905 - New CLI option -troeSslMode (env: ORIONLD_TROE_SSL_MODE) for TRoE Postgres SSL connections
  #XXXX - New CLI option -coreContextDir (env: ORIONLD_CORE_CONTEXT_DIR, default: /opt/orion/ldcontexts)
  #XXXX - Replaced prometheus-client-c with KPROM library for Prometheus metrics
  #XXXX - Default user @context served from local files, not only URLs
  #XXXX - MQTTS support - MQTT notifications over TLS (mqtts:// endpoint URIs)
  #XXXX - New CLI option -distOpTimeout (env: DIST_OP_TIMEOUT, default: 5000ms) for distributed operation timeout
  #XXXX - Experimental support for WebSockets (wip - Work In Progress)
  #XXXX - In-memory geo-fencing for subscriptions using GEOS (no DB round-trip for geo-matching)

### Pre-downloaded Core Context Files
  At startup the broker needs the NGSI-LD core context. Previously it was either found in
  the MongoDB context cache (from a previous run) or downloaded from https://uri.etsi.org.
  If both failed, a built-in hardcoded copy was used as last resort.

  A new step has been added between the MongoDB lookup and the network download:
  loading the core context from a local file on disk. The lookup order is now:

    1. MongoDB context cache (persisted from a previous run)
    2. Local file in -coreContextDir (new)
    3. Download from https://uri.etsi.org
    4. Built-in hardcoded copy (airgapped fallback)

  Each context file has the context URL on the first line, followed by the JSON-LD content.
  The filename is derived from the URL (e.g. ngsi-ld-core-context-v1.8.jsonld).
  Pre-downloaded files for all core context versions (v1.0 through v1.9) are shipped in
  the ldcontexts/ directory and copied to /opt/orion/ldcontexts/ in the Docker image.

  This eliminates startup failures caused by network timeouts when downloading the core
  context, which was particularly problematic in CI environments and cloud deployments.

## WebSockets (WIP - ETSI TC DATA has not yet finalized the WebSocket API)
  - WebSocket support is enabled via -wip ws
  - WS upgrade on /ws endpoint, with per-connection receive loop
  - Supported operations over WebSocket:
    - createEntity (POST /ngsi-ld/v1/entities)
    - createSubscription (POST /ngsi-ld/v1/subscriptions)
    - putEntity (PUT /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/{entityId})
    - patchEntity (PATCH /ngsi-ld/v1/entities/{entityId})
    - patchSubscription (PATCH /ngsi-ld/v1/subscriptions/{subscriptionId})
  - Notifications are sent over the WS connection for subscriptions created via WS
  - Message envelope format (same as MQTT): {"metadata": {...}, "body": {...}}

## DDS
  - Broker can now act as a client in DDS Actions
  - DDS Service Reply: ddsServiceReplyNotification support
  - DDS publish for already existing attributes in BATCH Update operations
  - Graceful DDS shutdown
  - Synchronous DDS semantics for PATCH /entities/{id} (when -wip dds is set); opt out with ?ddsSync=false
  - DDS Action goal lifecycle: an 'endpoint' sub-attribute spawns a temporary per-goal subscription that streams the goal's feedback/result/status to the initiator, torn down (instance + subscription) on goal completion
  - datasetId-aware notifications: a subscription's top-level 'datasetId' projects each notification body to the matching dataset instance(s) (NGSI-LD spec clause 8.5; '@none' selects the default instance) - so a goal's temp subscription delivers only that goal's instance
  - Concurrent action goals no longer cross-talk: each goal's temporary subscription watches "<attr>@<goalDatasetId>" (datasetId-scoped watchedAttributes), so it is TRIGGERED only by its own goal's instance changes, not by sibling goals modifying the same attribute
  - Fixed HTTP 500 on a second consecutive PATCH/attr roundtrip (dotForEq nested-Property names so mongo $set doesn't fragment them across path-separator dots)
  - Renamed reply envelope field xId to instanceHandleId (matches FastDDS InstanceHandle_t terminology, requested by eProsima)

